# ConflictLens — Environment Variables
#
# This file is committed to Git as a template. Copy it to .env and fill in real values.
# The real .env file is excluded from Git via .gitignore — never commit it.
#
# ── Who owns what ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# • VS Code extension:
#     Gemini API key is stored in VS Code settings as `conflictlens.geminiApiKey`
#     (set via File → Preferences → Settings in VS Code). That is the correct
#     location for an extension-side key — it does NOT belong in this .env file.
#
# • Local analysis backend (Node.js / Express engine):
#     Backend-side secrets live here. Fill these in your local .env file.
#
# ── Backend secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Port the local analysis engine listens on.
# The VS Code extension reads this via conflictlens.apiEndpoint (default: http://localhost:3000/analyze).
PORT=3000

# (Optional) If the backend makes its own calls to an AI API (e.g. Claude, Gemini)
# add the key here. Leave blank if the backend does not call any AI API directly.
# AI_API_KEY=

# ── Gemini Proxy (backend/gemini-proxy) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are for the hosted proxy server only — not the VS Code extension.
# See backend/gemini-proxy/.env.example for the full proxy-specific variable list.

# (Optional) Shared Gemini API key for the proxy server.
# GEMINI_PROXY_API_KEY=

# (Optional) Any other backend-specific environment variables go here.
# Example: database connection strings, feature flags, etc.
# DB_URL=
